  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/7/7c/Daredevil_Vol_1_1.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "A game of poker played inside of Fogwell's Gym is interrupted by a yellow-and-red costumed fighter who crashes the scene, demanding that the players tell him where he can find \"the Fixer.\"&nbsp; He proceeds to demonstrate that he's a force to be reckoned with before finally introducing himself as Daredevil.\n\nThe scene then shifts to a flashback to 1950, where 8-year-old Matt Murdock is being told by his father \"Battling Murdock\" how important it is to study.&nbsp; The older Murdock promised Matt's mother before she died that he wouldn't let their son \"grow up to be an uneducated pug like me!\"&nbsp; He wants better things for his son, even though Matt seems proud of his father.&nbsp; But the dad is past his prime now.\n\nAnd so Matt becomes the top student in his class, forsaking all sports, even though he longs to participate.&nbsp; His constant studies earn him the nickname \"Daredevil\" by the crueler kids who laugh at him and think he's a sissy.&nbsp; So he begins to train in the privacy of his home using his dad's many exercise machines and equipment.&nbsp; But he never forgets the ultimate goal -- to be the best student he can.&nbsp; His dad is thrilled and tells him, \"the day will come when you'll <i>thank</i> me, boy!&nbsp; You're gonna <i>amount</i> to something... just the way your mother would've <i>wanted</i> you to!\"\n\nBut the dad is haunted by his advancing age, hoping he'll get enough work -- to keep fighting -- until he can pay his son's way through college.&nbsp; \"I <i>owe</i> him that... for the way he's worked all these years!\"&nbsp; But as he's now \"washed up,\" he has no choice but to turn to someone like the \"Fixer\" -- someone he's always sworn he'd steer clear of.&nbsp; But he has no choice.&nbsp; \"I <i>have</i> to get a fight!\"\n\nHe reluctantly signs a contract with the Fixer, but when he returns home to tell Matt that he'll now be able to send him to college, Matt is not there.&nbsp; Matt is on his way home from the library , sees a blind man trying to cross the street, and moves to help, \"...taking the most important few steps of his entire life!\"&nbsp; He sees a truck (labeled \"Ajax Atomic Labs Radio-Active Materials\") barreling toward the man.&nbsp; He jumps into action and is able to throw the man out of the truck's path, but he himself is hit, and \"a cylinder fell from the truck... it struck his face!\"\n\nMatt has lost his sight, but the doctors have hope that an operation might one day restore it.&nbsp; In the meantime, he promises his dad he will continue his studies and get his diploma.&nbsp; But he soon finds that...\"Ever since my accident, I seem able to do everything lots better than before... even <i>without</i> my sight!&nbsp; It's as though nature made all my senses far more powerful, to compensate for my blindness!\"&nbsp; He concludes that it must have been the radioactive materials in the truck!\n\nAnd soon, as he promised, he graduates from high school and proceeds to college, sharing a dorm room with a new friend, Franklin \"Foggy\" Nelson.&nbsp; His studies now seem even easier than ever.&nbsp; \"<i>Everything</i> seems easy for me now!&nbsp; All my senses are razor sharp!\"&nbsp; (The story then highlights what he can do with his four remaining senses.)&nbsp; But his most important new ability is what appears to be a \"radar\" sense that allows him to navigate everything without bumping into things.\n\nAll the while, Battling Murdock seems to have come upon a stroke of good fortune, winning fights right and left.&nbsp; But he has no idea that it's all because the Fixer has set up every one of them, paying his opponents to \"take a dive.\"&nbsp; All this serves to build him up... but the day of reckoning is coming now!\n\nSoon, Foggy approaches Matt on campus, excited to read in the newspaper that Murdock is set to face Davis, the #2 contender.&nbsp; Matt bought tickets to the bout already.&nbsp; But Battling Murdock has been told to take a dive in the first round that night.\n\nHowever, Murdock sees his son in the crowd, there to root for him.&nbsp; He refuses to disappoint him.&nbsp; And he begins the fight of his life, while from the sideline he hears the Fixer calling for him to go down, but he refuses!&nbsp; \"It's my one chance!&nbsp; Maybe my last chance... to do something to make my son <i>proud</i> of me!&nbsp; I'm not gonna fail him!&nbsp; I'm gonna win!\"&nbsp; <b>And he does!</b>\n\nIn the dressing room afterward, Matt and Foggy congratulate the older Murdock.&nbsp; \"I wanted you to be proud of me, Matt... my son!\"&nbsp; But the Fixer is nearby in a dark sedan, seething about the double-cross.&nbsp; And moments later, outside the gym, with a [SOUNDEFFECT: text=CRACK!] the Fixer's man Slade makes sure that Murdock has had his <i>final</i> triumph!\n\nMatt grieves for his father, but he'll be graduating soon, and Foggy wants them to be partners via an office that his wealthy father is setting up.&nbsp; Thus, \"Nelson and Murdock, Attorneys at Law\" is born!&nbsp; Their new secretary is Karen Page, and Matt is stricken with her right away.&nbsp; (We read later in the issue that she's a stranger to New York and so chooses to spend her free time at the office.&nbsp; And she even says to Foggy later, about Matt -- \"he seems to need someone to look after him!\")\n\nBut his dad's murder remains foremost on Matt's mind.&nbsp; He promised his dad years ago that he would use his <i>head</i> and never become a fighter, depending only on his strength.&nbsp; But his new abilities surely would be of <i>some</i> aid, so he decides to create an alter-ego to allow Matt Murdock to remain true to his promise.&nbsp; He creates a costume and decides to use the name the kids on his block used to deride him years earlier -- <b>Daredevil</b>!&nbsp; And a modified version of his cane will be his weapon (which he later refers to as a \"billy club\")!\n\nHe starts out by tracking down his father's manager -- the \"Fixer.\"&nbsp; Daredevil confronts the Fixer, who tells his boys to attack, and we soon watch as he navigates through them using his enhanced senses.&nbsp; With his boys down, DD orders the Fixer to tell him if he had anything to do with Murdock's death -- and the change in his pulse rate indicates he's lying about it.\n\nSlade and the Fixer manage to briefly get away to the street below.&nbsp; And having changed back again to Matt Murdock, the blind man is easily able to follow the smell of the Fixer's cigar down the road.&nbsp; They make it to the subway, where they plan to slip away, but they discover Daredevil now blocking their path.&nbsp; DD takes down Slade, the guy who actually pulled the trigger in his father's death, but as he pursues the Fixer (who <i>ordered</i> the death), the latter suffers from a sudden heart attack and dies.&nbsp; The police arrive and haul off Slade after Daredevil manipulates him into confessing.\n\nAs Matt arrives back at his office, he finds that an accused murderer named Slade has called and asked them to defend him!&nbsp; But Foggy turned him down, convinced from the police report that he is guilty.&nbsp; He hopes Matt doesn't mind!&nbsp; He doesn't.\n\nMatt stands by the window and thinks, \"Dad, wherever you are... I kinda hope you're resting easier now!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "There's not much to find fault with here.&nbsp; From start to finish, this book, both the current events, but <i>especially </i>the flashback origin, are about as compelling an introduction story as has been pulled off by Marvel to this point.&nbsp; (Mind you, as I discuss later, it's not the <b>best</b> origin story, but it <i>is</i> probably the most <i>fleshed-out</i> one from a start-to-finish perspective.)\n\n[ASSET: left | CallMeDaredevil | width=6%] The main character is introduced early (page 2), though no hint of what makes him unique is provided -- only that he's obviously a very skilled fighter, <i>easily </i>able to make short work of four poker players, all the while demanding information about where he might find someone named \"the Fixer.\"\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThis is where the story completely changes, and the flashback that begins in 1950 is presented.\n\n[ASSET: right | CompellingFlashback | width=28%] It doesn't take long at all for readers to learn that, far from this being a mere four-and-a-half pages (as we saw back in [LINK:1961:FF_1|FF #1]), this flashback takes up about <b>half the entire book</b> and gives a very nice introduction to both a young Matt Murdock and his middle-age, past-his-prime boxer fighter, a widower who promised his wife he would push Matt to make something great out of himself, to not allow him to become \"an uneducated pug like me!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=<i>You're </i>going to amount to something, Matt!\"]\n\nIt doesn't matter that the young Matt is <i>already</i> is proud of his dad and thinks he's the greatest:&nbsp; the dad only views his profession as a dead-end job and wants <i>more</i> for his son.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=This is compelling stuff] that comes from what boils down to a handful of introductory panels\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; text=From the very first flashback page, writer Stan Lee already has our attention... and our empathy!]\n\nEven as we see how Matt's dedication to study costs him time he could be spending playing sports (and draws ridicule from his fellow students, who mockingly refer to him as \"Daredevil\"), Matt's decision to add physical training to his daily routine ensure that both his mind <i>and</i> body are sharpened.\n\nOf course, I don't need to describe the sequence of events any further, because you can either read it for yourself or scan the plot summary above.&nbsp; Matt is blinded, hit by a radioactive cylinder, and develops enhanced senses.\n[ASSET: center | RadioactiveTruck | width=large]\n\nSuffice it to say that by the time Matt and Foggy are ring-side on page 12, and his father sees his son in the crowd, he fatefully decides that doing his best in front of his son is worth <b>far more</b> than an order from the Fixer to take a dive, [SPAN: severity=error; text=a decision that ultimately costs him his life.]\n[ASSET: center | OneFinalMomentOfGlory | width=large]\n\nBut the panels on page 12 showing Murdock in the ring, knowing that his son was present (even if he couldn't watch)?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=It was emotionally compelling], and I found myself lost in the story.\n\nIn other words... [SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=pure gold!]\n\n[ASSET: left | IllBeDaredevil | width=15%] The flashback continues... Matt graduates from college and joins his friend Foggy in New York City in their new law firm of <i>Nelson and Murdock</i>.&nbsp; Karen Page is introduced, and Matt finally decides to go after his father's killer in his new outfit as \"Daredevil!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] That brings us back to present day, where the story began... and I have to say, [SPAN: severity=success; text=there was no putting this book down!]\n\nThe fact that the hero is blind <b>is almost beside the point</b>, because the real heart of the issue is Matt's vendetta against his father's killer.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=That's what ultimately matters], even though Stan begins sprinkling in thoughts that the title hero is having that help shed light on how he's managing his surroundings without sight.\n\nI have to admit -- [SPAN: severity=warning; text=these thought bubbles were a little <i>distracting</i> at first] -- and I have a hard time believing that such a technique can carry a book over the long haul without getting tedius -- but in a [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=first issue featuring a blind hero?]&nbsp; I think it was okay and helped sell the blindness.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nObviously the last pages of the story tied a neat bow around both the Fixer (who ordered the hit on Matt's father) and his henchman, Slade (who actually pulled the trigger).&nbsp; Daredevil introduces himself to the police and hints that they'll be seeing him around town.\n\nBoth Foggy and Karen were given a small bit of screen time as well, and Matt returns to the office, ready to begin his new life, having wrapped up all his old loose ends.\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=All is well!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | GoldenAgeFace | width=12%] As for the artwork (and I have more to say about this in the Decoder Ring section), I found Bill Everett's work [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=<i>perfect</i>ly suited for the character], with my only comment being that there were a couple of panels (like the one on the left) that reminded me of comics from the Golden Age, which isn't really surprising, seeing as how Bill actually <i>worked</i> on some those old series.\n[BR]\n[BR] The faces in some of the panels seemed <i>very</i> reminiscent of those commonly seen during that era.\n\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nTo do this story justice, I feel I need to temporarily remove my [PAGE: scenario | Scenario] hat for a moment, because this is a rare occasion where I feel my \"future knowledge\" adds vital justification for why the story received the grade it did.\n\nMy first exposure to this origin story was in the Roger McKenzie/Frank Miller re-telling in <i>Daredevil</i> 164 (DD #164), which was published soon after I began collecting comics.&nbsp; I had long assumed they had added many little \"extras\" to the tale, as we'd seen done with the Spider-Man origin.\n\nAnd while that is certainly still <i>true</i> (for example, Battling Murdock's first name of \"Jack\" [SPAN: severity=warning; text=never appears in this issue]), I found the original version stocked with a wealth of detail and emotional impact [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=that I had long assumed absent] due to the lack-of-space problems that seemed to plague most Silver Age origin stories.\n\nOn the contrary, [SPAN: severity=success; text=this one felt far more <i>complete</i> than any previous one.]&nbsp; If anything, I think it hints that the introduction of Daredevil brought with it a careful attention to detail that many of those earlier Silver Age origins never got (even though I maintain that Spider-Man's origin story is best, but it was <i>also</i> only 11 pages).\n\nConsequently, I was blown away by the experience of reading this... the <i>original</i>... version for the very first time...\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=Everything I remembered... <b>it was all here from the start</b>...]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(That speaks <i>volumes</i> about the care and forethought...)]\n\n(Scenario hat back on now...)\n\n[DIVIDER]\nFor an introductory issue, I maintain that, while there's <i>certainly</i> more for us to learn about the title hero (which is a good thing), the backstory involving a father who wanted his son to become more than just another uneducated fighter, to \"<i>amount </i>to something\" -- this wasn't just <i>relatable</i> -- Stan's dialogue made it completely <b>believable</b>.\n\nSadly, Battling Murdock [SPAN: severity=error; text=paid the ultimate price] for his final moment to make his boy proud of him.&nbsp; It wasn't quite the gut-punch of seeing Peter Parker come face-to-face with his uncle's murderer, since the older Murdock <i>had</i> to have known that there would be retribution... [SPAN: severity=info; text=and yet did it <i>anyway</i> for the chance to witness the look on his son's face.]\n\nMatt's story too is largely relatable, a boy who wanted to make his father proud of him and did the right thing, putting his studies first while at the same time taking advantage of the equipment around him to hone his physical skills.&nbsp; The introductions of Foggy at college and later Karen at the office were mostly well done (though the attraction between Matt and Karen could have waited an issue or two to let it develop a bit more organically).\n\nIf there are any negatives at all to be found, it's the unfortunate re-use of radioactivity as the cause of Matt's new abilities (which was brushed over so quickly that we almost forget about it).\n\nBut more importantly -- [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=that horrible ketchup and mustard costume!]\n\nI totally <i>understand</i> that it was designed by a blind man, making it easy to explain away the lack of fashion, but <b><i>surely</i>, the creators of the magazine must be blamed instead!</b>\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Come on, guys!]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Heads must <i>roll</i> for this travesty!)]\n\nBut... getting back to the negatives, the fact that there was immediate (and <i>needless</i>) chemistry between Karen and Matt right from the start also played a role in holding this one back from full NM status.\n\nI still believe the story within [LINK:1963:ASM_10|ASM #10|2027-04-05] was overall <i>better</i> than this tale (hence its higher grade), but the story and origin aren't the only factors -- the <i>cumulative</i> effect of the interesting new character and his backstory push this one slightly ahead of virtually everything that Marvel has published thus far, and I award it almost full marks.\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Well done!]",
  "side_note": [
    "[ASSET: left | EightIn1950 | width=24%] In 1964, we can assume Matt is 22 years-old, having been eight years old in 1950, as indicated in the narration on page 5.\n\nYeah, it seems highly unlikely that an accused murderer would immediately give a brand-new law firm the call for legal defense, with two lawyers fresh out of college, so that too pulls this one down from NM status."
  ],
  "decoder_ring": "[HANG0] This is the most careful reading I've ever given to the origin of Daredevil, and while I know the story behind Battling Murdock is well-documented, I do now wonder whether Marvel ever plumbed the depths of his <i>mother</i>.&nbsp; Though page 5 indicates that she died, we still aren't told anything about her.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=I suppose I'll just keep reading and perhaps learn more!]\n\n[HANG0] I would be totally remiss if I did not at least acknowledge the art of Bill Everett, who only did this one issue before realizing that there was no way he could commit to it on a regular basis (the <i>Special Announcements Section</i> of FF #27 explains more about why).\n[BR]\n[BR] Obviously a veteran of comics, he is credited with the creation of the Golden Age Sub-Mariner.&nbsp; I thought his artwork was quite satisfactory and captured the motion of the action sequences well, and his other figures were good too and in no way distracted from the story.&nbsp; I was sad when I learned that this was to be his only issue.\n[BR]\n[BR] Thankfully, we haven't seen the last of Bill Everett, whose art appears here and there in the years ahead.&nbsp; And of course, he'll eventually be back on the <i>Sub-Mariner</i> series later on, so good for him!",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}